        {
          "@id": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/process/Process_CiHealth",
          "@type": "bootstrap:Process",
          "conformsTo": [
            "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/externalSchema/omg-bpmn-2.0",
            "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/externalSchema/omg-dd-1.0",
            "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/externalSchema/w3c-xsd11-structures"
          ],
          "depiction": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/assets/img/workflows/ci-health-watch.svg",
          "description": "Is CI actually working on the default branch: record each workflow's state on one tracking issue, and fail the job only when the state could not be determined. A WORKFLOW'S OUTCOME IS INVISIBLE FROM A CHECKOUT. Bean `xom7`: one workflow here fired on every push to `main` and FAILED ALL 30 TIMES OVER TWO MONTHS with nothing in the repository saying so. This is the backstop.\n\nWEEKLY, NOT DAILY, and the cadence is an argument rather than a default: the defect is \"nobody noticed for two months\", not \"nobody noticed for a day\", and the session-start sweep already covers every day somebody is working. A weekly run bounds the blind spot to seven days without turning the tracking issue into a feed.\n\n`Could we tell?` IS NOT SIMPLY THE EXIT CODE, and that is the subtlety worth drawing. `bun` exits 1 on an uncaught exception as well as on a live failure, so exit 1 alone does not separate \"found something red\" from \"crashed\". The REPORT FILE separates them: `--out` writes it before any exit path, so a genuine red always has one and a crash before that point does not. Exit 1 with no report is reclassified as `unknown`. Without that, a crash would open a tracking issue quoting a stale file.\n\nTHE THIRD STATE IS THE WHOLE POINT, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE THAT FAILS THE JOB. Verified 2026-09-20 by reading the `if:` guards: `unknown` runs `exit 1`; a finding does NOT. A repository in trouble leaves this workflow GREEN and speaks through the tracking issue, while a sweep that could not look turns the job red. That inversion is deliberate and is invisible from the run list, which is the argument for drawing it.\n\nONE TRACKING ISSUE, EDITED IN PLACE, never a comment and never a second issue — a feed nobody reads is the failure mode a watchdog invites. On `unknown` the issue is left UNTOUCHED: not closed, not opened, not edited. Closing it would assert the thing the sweep just failed to establish.\n\n`Ensure the tracking label exists` sits AFTER `Could we tell?` and before the split, because both issue paths need the label — the clean path filters on it too, and on a repository where nothing has ever gone wrong it would not otherwise exist. It is skipped on `unknown`, where neither path runs and a label hiccup must not be what fails the job.\n\nThe verdict is reported through the shared `tracked-watch` process (Process_TrackedWatch), with the label `ci-health`. Its three ends say what happened: unknown (job red, issue untouched), clean (issue closed), or found (issue opened or edited, job green).",
          "enforcement": "strict",
          "flowCount": 3,
          "inSubgraph": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/directory/sdlc",
          "name": "Is CI actually working on the default branch?",
          "nodeCount": 4,
          "sourcePath": "sdlc/processes/ci-health-watch.bpmn",
          "sourceUrl": "https://github.com/litlfred/cat-harness/blob/main/sdlc/processes/ci-health-watch.bpmn",
          "startNode": "https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/cat-harness/process/Process_CiHealth/node/Start_Sweep",
          "summary": "Is CI actually working on the default branch: record each workflow's state on one tracking issue, and fail the job only when the state could not be determined."
        },
